  <title>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Strengthened institutional capacity of workers&apos; organizations</narrative>
  </title>
  <description type="1">
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">In recent years, India has navigated multiple global shocks, including the COVID-19 pandemic and escalating impacts of climate change. These challenges, compounded by rapid digital transformation and platformization of the economy, have reshaped employment patterns and labour relations. Despite global economic pressures, India maintained a respectable GDP growth of around 7% in 2023. However, pandemic-induced disruptions such as stagnant gender pay gaps, rising self-employment due to economic stress, reversal towards agricultural employment, and persistent unemployment have been an area of concern. Structural transformations, particularly in the gig and platform economy, outpace existing labour protections. Regional disparities fuel distress migration, highlighting the need for inclusive growth strategies. 
The ILO collaborates with 11 national trade unions in India, five of which are recognized as most representative. Trade unions have expanded their membership, notably within the informal economy, focusing on youth, women, migrants, and gig workers. In response, the ILO&#x2019;s 2024-25 strategy aligns with the Decent Work Country Programme (DWCP) 2023-2027 and the UN Sustainable Development Cooperation Framework (UNSDCF). It emphasizes supporting trade unions through capacity-building for just transition, specialized training on International Labour Standards (ILS), fostering engagement in developmental processes, advancing labour protections in emerging sectors, and promoting digital strategies via Workers&#x2019; Information and Support Centres (WISCs). Cross-cutting priorities include enhancing social dialogue, increasing participation of women and youth, and encouraging joint and encouraging joint trade union actions.</narrative>
  </description>
